    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the full test suite is green (`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class; both tests pass, BUILD SUCCESS).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe create-owner pipeline (`owners.POST.yml`: `validate \u2192 build \u2192 save \u2192 respond`) binds and republishes the request body in its first step, `ValidateOwnerFields`. That's the right place to normalize, since it runs before the entity is built/persisted and it's the only step that reads `@RequestBody` (no `@Valid`, so the DTO's `^[0-9]*$` pattern isn't enforced at binding).\n\n1. **`ValidateOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 after the existing blank-field checks, strip every non-digit from the telephone (`replaceAll(\"\\\\D\", \"\")`), require exactly 10 digits, then write the normalized value back onto the DTO before publishing it. The mapper copies that DTO into the `Owner` entity, so `\"0412345678\"` is stored and returned. If the stripped value isn't exactly 10 digits, it throws a new checked exception.\n\n2. **`InvalidOwnerTelephoneException.java`** (new, `function/owner`) \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it appears in the `throws` clause and is routed by OfficeFloor escalation.\n\n3. **`InvalidOwnerTelephoneExceptionHandler.java`** (new, `escalation`) \u2014 responds `400` with `{\"errors\":[\"telephone\"]}`, mirroring the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler` style.\n\n4. **`...InvalidOwnerTelephoneException.yml`** (new, `officefloor/escalation/`) \u2014 maps the exception to its handler.\n\nVerified against the two spec cases: `\"(04) 1234-5678\"` \u2192 stored/returned as `\"0412345678\"` (200-family + readable via GET), and `\"12345\"` \u2192 `400`.\n\nNote: the entity's `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` still enforces the 10-digit invariant on persist as a backstop; normalization makes valid input pass it, and the too-few-digits case is rejected earlier with a clean 400.",
